  "Question": "A square and a circle have equal perimeters. The ratio of the area of the circle to the area of the square is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac{4}{\\pi}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac{\\pi}{\\sqrt2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\frac{4}{1}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{\\pi}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Let \\(s>0\\) be the square's side length and \\(r>0\\) the circle's radius. Equal perimeters connect these dimensions:\n\\[\n4s=2\\pi r,\n\\qquad r=\\frac{2s}{\\pi}.\n\\]\nThe circle has area \\(\\pi r^2\\), and the square has area \\(s^2\\). Therefore the requested ratio is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi r^2}{s^2}\n=\\frac{\\pi(2s/\\pi)^2}{s^2}\n=\\frac4\\pi.\n\\]\nBecause \\(s>0\\), canceling \\(s^2\\) is valid. The ratio is \\(\\boxed{4/\\pi}\\), choice A.",
